You can replace the implicit stack used by recursion with an explicit stack data structure. 

Example: Recursive depth‑first traversal of a binary tree.

Recursive version:

```java
void dfs(Node node){
    if(node==null) return;
    System.out.println(node.val);
    dfs(node.left);
    dfs(node.right);
}
```

Iterative version using a `Stack`:

```java
void dfsIterative(Node root){
    if(root==null) return;
    Stack<Node> stack = new Stack<>();
    stack.push(root);
    while(!stack.isEmpty()){
        Node cur = stack.pop();
        System.out.println(cur.val);
        if(cur.right!=null) stack.push(cur.right);
        if(cur.left!=null) stack.push(cur.left);
    }
}
```

Both produce the same preorder traversal.

Each recursive call consumes a frame on the call stack. If the recursion depth exceeds the stack size, a `StackOverflowError` (Java) or similar exception occurs.

Prevention techniques:
1. **Tail recursion**: Some languages optimize tail calls to reuse the same stack frame.
2. **Iterative approach**: Use loops or explicit stacks.
3. **Limit depth**: Add a depth counter and throw a controlled exception.
4. **Increase stack size**: Not ideal; e.g., `java -Xss2m`.

Example: Computing the nth Fibonacci number recursively can overflow for large n. An iterative loop solves it.

```c
int fib(int n){
    if(n<=1) return n;
    int a=0, b=1;
    for(int i=2;i<=n;i++){
        int tmp = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = tmp;
    }
    return b;
}
```

Recursive inorder traversal:

```java
void inorder(Node node){
    if(node==null) return;
    inorder(node.left);
    System.out.println(node.val);
    inorder(node.right);
}
```

Iterative inorder traversal using a stack:

```java
void inorderIter(Node root){
    Stack<Node> stack = new Stack<>();
    Node cur = root;
    while(cur != null || !stack.isEmpty()){
        while(cur != null){
            stack.push(cur);
            cur = cur.left;
        }
        cur = stack.pop();
        System.out.println(cur.val);
        cur = cur.right;
    }
}
```

Both visit nodes in the same left‑root‑right order.

